  "body": "### Description\r\n\r\nKicking a non-existent user doesn't return a 403 as [the spec suggests it should](https://matrix.org/docs/spec/client_server/r0.3.0.html#id206). The server accepts the message and sends out a `\"membership\": \"leave\"` event with the `state_key` referring to the non-existent user (possibly including whitespace).\r\n\r\nThis confuses at least Riot desktop, producing the following outcome even though the user `foo@bar.baz` isn't in the room:\r\n\r\n![foo@bar.baz leaves the room, even though he doesn't exist](https://i.imgur.com/x6fG40o.png)\r\n\r\nFurthermore, whitespace can be embedded in the `user_id` when executing the kick, making the problem even worse because you can spoof very realistic looking but fake events about real users. For instance, by kicking user `@nonexistent:example.com joined, @realuser:example.com`, you can make Riot produce a message making it appear as if a non-existent user joined and some other real user left the room, while nothing actually happened: ![](https://i.imgur.com/S9RbYvs.png)\r\n\r\n### Steps to reproduce\r\n\r\n* Send a `POST` to `<server>/_matrix/client/r0/rooms/<room>/kick?access_token=<access_token>` with `{\"user_id\":\"@nonexistent:example.com\"}` in the body.\r\n\r\n#### Expected:\r\nThe server returns a 403 and doesn't send out a \"leave\" event.\r\n\r\n#### Observed: \r\nThe server accepts the event and does send out a \"leave\" event.\r\n\r\n### Version information\r\n\r\nThis was tested on our homeserver, running Synapse 0.26.0 on FreeBSD 11.1, installed using FreeBSD's `pkg`. The package is `py27-matrix-synapse-0.26.0`.",
